@import "./compiler.sly"
@use Project

class SlytherProject {
    A Slyther project is a folder where the slyther files live. It is created with the root, a
    #{Build::SlytherGenerator}, where to report progress, the verbose option, which wraps the generator in
    a #{Build::SlytherTracedGenerator}, and the folder its code lives in, `.slyther/src` when none is given.
    It contains the following structure:

    - main.sly: Entry point
    - .slyther: Contains the Slyther output files
    - .slyther/src: The code of the project, unless an `@src` line moves it elsewhere, which is why nothing
      else is ever written next to it. Every script, operation and LLM runs from it, so a `./` read by the
      code is really that folder, and the paths the rules of a kind give are relative to it. Wherever it
      sits, whatever runs from it is handed the artifacts folder as the way there from it, never as a fixed
      `../artifacts`, so moving the code never leaves an operation pointing at nothing
    - .slyther/build
    - .slyther/build/parser.json: The JSON representation of the SlytherScript parsed from main.sly after a build
    - .slyther/packages/name: The packages installed, each the folder a package manager copied, whose
      main.sly an `@import "name"` reads, see #{Compiler::SlytherParser}
    - .slyther/artifacts: The operations of every kind as built, see #{Build::SlytherArtifactBuilder}
    - .slyther/artifacts/manifest.json: The #{Build::SlytherArtifactManifest}
    - .slyther/instances/manifest.json: The #{Check::SlytherInstanceManifest}
    - .slyther/instances/expanded/kind/name.sly: What the instance of a composite kind emitted, see
      #{Check::SlytherInstanceChecker}

    .slyther/build is ignored by version control, .slyther/artifacts, .slyther/instances and
    .slyther/packages are committed, so a checkout runs the operations without building them again and a
    check evaluates only what changed.

    The packages are committed because what they hold is an input of everything that is: the prose of a
    packaged kind is in the closure hash of every artifact built from it, so a checkout without them
    holds the scripts and not what they were built from, and its main.sly does not even parse. They are
    prose, of the size of prose, and the diff of an upgrade is what explains the scripts that changed
    with it. Ignoring them would only be worth it against a registry that serves a version again by its
    hash, which a folder a package manager copied from the machine it was published on is not.

    Its static `of` takes the root and what the constructor takes besides the folder of code, parses the
    entry point with #{Compiler::SlytherParser} and returns the project pointed at the folder its `@src`
    declares, keeping the #{Compiler::ParsedSlytherScript} it read so a build does not read it twice. Every
    command makes its project with it, so the folder a build writes to is the one a run and an operation
    run from, without either being told where it is: nothing but the scripts ever decides it. It must throw
    an error when the entry point cannot be read, so a command outside a project says so instead of
    working on a folder that is not one.
}

method SlytherProject::init {
    Initializes a project in the given folder, the current one when none is given, creating whatever part
    of its structure is missing and never touching what already exists, and returns the project and the
    paths it created. It creates the entry point and the output folder, and never the folder of code: an
    empty entry point declares no `@src` yet, so where the code goes is not known until something parses
    it, and the first build creates it wherever it is by then declared. Since it is what makes a project,
    it is the one thing that does not go through the static `of` of #{SlytherProject}.
}

method SlytherProject::build {
    Compiles the project, given the max option:

    Executes the #{SlytherProject::parse} method
    Executes the #{SlytherProject::buildArtifacts} method
    Executes the #{SlytherProject::checkInstances} method with compile, so what is missing is created and what
    is outdated or fails is updated

    Returns what it did to every file and what happened to every instance
}

method SlytherProject::parse {
    Writes the JSON representation of the #{Compiler::ParsedSlytherScript} the project was made with to:
    .slyther/build/parser.json
}

method SlytherProject::buildArtifacts {
    Groups the parsed script into every #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind} and every #{Compiler::SlytherRunScript},
    prints their warnings, and builds their operations and scripts into .slyther/artifacts with
    #{Build::SlytherArtifactBuilder}, returning what it did to every file: built, rebuilt, kept or removed

    Before it builds anything, it makes sure every role the work of the project asks for is played: the
    role of every step and of every operation of its kinds, and the scribe and the reviewer that write
    and review its scripts when it has any. Whatever the generator says keeps it from asking for that work
    throws, every problem at once, and a role played by the standard role of its line because nobody
    binds it is warned about, see #{Compiler::SlytherRole}. Then it pins who plays each of those roles with
    the generator, once per build, so every call of the build asks the same model even when an alias
    starts resolving to another halfway, and what is recorded, who judged and who wrote, names what was
    really asked. It says what every alias resolved to, and, against what it resolved to the build before,
    kept in .slyther/build/roles.json, when it now resolves to another: what a judge gave then is
    evaluated again, since who judges is part of what evaluates an instance.
}

method SlytherProject::run {
    Runs a #{Compiler::SlytherRunScript} as built, given its name, `default` when none is given, and its args,
    which must fit its params, otherwise it must throw an error, as must a script that is not built. Its
    steps run in order from .slyther/src, with the args as positional arguments and the terminal as their
    stdin, stdout and stderr, and it stops at the first that exits with a code other than 0, returning
    that code.
}

method SlytherProject::runOperation {
    Runs an operation of a kind as built, given the kind, the operation and its args, which must fit the
    params of the operation, otherwise it must throw an error, as must an operation that is not built.

    A deterministic operation runs the script of each of its steps in order from .slyther/src,
    with the args as positional arguments, and stops at the first that exits with a code other than 0,
    returning that code. An operation that is not deterministic returns the markdown that orchestrates it
    with every `{param}` substituted by its arg, or, when a #{Build::SlytherGenerator} is given to execute
    it, hands the markdown to it and returns its reply.
}
method SlytherProject::check {
    Checks the project without touching its code, given the max option:

    Executes the #{SlytherProject::parse} method
    Executes the #{SlytherProject::buildArtifacts} method
    Executes the #{SlytherProject::checkInstances} method without compile
}

method SlytherProject::checkInstances {
    Checks every instance the parsed script declares with #{Check::SlytherInstanceChecker}, given the compile
    and max options, and returns what happened to every one: created, updated, fixed, pass, fail, missing,
    kept, expanded or orphan, with its reason, its errors and its parent when it was emitted
}
